Things to try: the pin line has slope 5 dB per decade (thermal noise ∝ \(\sqrt{B_e}\)), the quantum limit 10 dB per decade (fixed photons per bit). The pin receiver is 20–35 dB above the quantum limit at all rates, because the thermal noise of even a good amplifier corresponds to thousands of photons per bit. Lower the amplifier noise to 1 pA/√Hz: the pin improves by 10 dB. Set \(k=0\) for the APD: it gains another 9 dB, because the gain becomes almost noiseless (\(F o2\)). The preamplified receiver is within 9–14 dB of the quantum limit almost independently of the electronics; narrowing the optical filter towards the signal bandwidth helps. Lowering the BER target to \(10^{-3}\) (with forward error correction, the norm today) halves Q and saves about 3 dB for the thermal-noise-limited pin.

